; Regression: a QUALIFIED STATIC call with too many arguments used to compile
; clean (2026-08-15).
;
; `Type.method(args)` on a this-less member resolves through the s155 static
; path, which took the FIRST same-named member and never compared its parameter
; count with the call's. So `Box.twice(1, 2, 3)` on a one-parameter static
; planned with rc 0 and handed the two surplus arguments to a signature that has
; no slots for them — and emit_call synthesizes the callee's type from the
; ARGUMENTS, so LLVM had nothing to verify against either.
;
; Every neighbouring call shape already had the gate: a `this` method through
; lookup_method#, a free function through check_function_match# (an arity
; mismatch loses the candidate and surfaces as "function not found"), and a
; namespace function with an explicit `this` through the arity_ok branch above
; the static path. The qualified static was the one direction left silent.
;
; Only TOO MANY is silent at all. Too few also loses the arity test, but the
; call then has no candidate anywhere and is already reported.
;
; xfail: wrong number of arguments for Box.twice: got 3, expected 1

define Box
(
    v: int
)
{
    function twice(a: int) returns int
    {
        a * 2
    }
}

let r Box.twice(1, 2, 3)
print "unreachable"
